From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 03:10:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 41/49] fix(canvas): a frame keeps its picture through a zoom's
 settle

When a zoom came to rest, frames changed how they are drawn: culled frames
went live (a FrameView over the GPU canvas or over their shell), live ones
went back to shells, and every preview switched to the level of the new
zoom. Each of those started from an empty <img>, so the frame showed its
white surface for a frame or more before the picture arrived. Seen as a white
flash on every zoom in or out, worst on large frames.

What drew the frame stays until its replacement has drawn, as a map keeps a
tile until the new one has loaded:

- GPU canvas -> FrameView: the canvas keeps drawing the frame under its
  FrameView (gpuScene's `under`) while the view is transparent, until the
  view shows its picture (decoded) or its document; two animation frames
  later the store's `painted` lets the canvas drop it. 1.5 s at the most.
- Shell -> FrameView: FrameSlot keeps the FrameShell mounted beneath the
  new view on the same terms.
- A preview whose URL changes (another level, a new version) keeps the
  picture it showed beneath the new <img> until that one has decoded;
  Chromium paints an <img> whose src changed as empty meanwhile.
- A new preview of a frame (FrameView -> shell) starts with the frame's
  last shown picture beneath it, and a URL this page has loaded is eager
  and sync-decoded, so it paints in its first frame (the HTML spec's list
  of available images) instead of waiting for the lazy-load pass.

Measured with a screencast of ctrl+wheel zooms (0.15>0.8, 0.8>0.15,
0.15>0.6, 0.6>0.2), flagging a frame whose white share jumps over the
frames around it, two runs per renderer, software GL:

  renderer                 before                     after
  GPU + zoom pictures      62% white at the settle    0 flags in 8 zooms
                           in 3 of 4 runs, 1-2 more
  GPU                      1-2 flags per run          0 in 8
  DOM + zoom pictures      1-3 per run                1 in 8 (mid-gesture,
                                                      on a stalled run)
  DOM                      ~80% white zooming in      0 in 8
